/* ==============================================================
   02 — TRF: the Tool Reference Frame
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   SetTrf — telling the robot where the tool tip is,
             and which way it points
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed. Runs safely with no tool.
   WATCH     A rotation about the flange, then about the
             fingertips
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE OTHER HALF OF THE FRAME STORY

   The WRF answers "where is the work?". The TRF answers "where
   is the tool?".

       SetTrf(x, y, z, alpha, beta, gamma)

   defines the TRF — the Tool Reference Frame — with respect to
   the FRF, the Flange Reference Frame at the end of the arm.

   By default the TRF sits exactly on the flange. That is
   correct and useless: nothing does work at the flange. The work
   happens at the tip of the gripper finger, the point of the
   needle, the centre of the suction cup — typically 60 to 120 mm
   further out, and often off-axis.

   Tell the robot where that point is, and two things become
   true:

   1. Every pose you write refers to the TOOL TIP, not the
      flange. You program the point that touches the part.

   2. Rotations happen ABOUT THE TIP. This is the one that
      matters and the one that demonstrates well. Ask the robot
      to rotate the tool 30 degrees and it pivots around the
      point of the needle, instead of swinging the needle through
      an arc as the flange rotates.

   THE TCP

   The origin of the TRF is the TCP — Tool Centre Point. When a
   customer says "our TCP", this command is what they mean. (Not
   to be confused with TCP/IP, which is unfortunate but
   universal.)

   WHICH WAY THE AXES POINT

   A TRF is a position AND an orientation, and the orientation
   is worth choosing on purpose. The convention used from here
   on — and the common one in industry:

     z   out of the tool, the way it works: through the
         fingertips, out of the nozzle
     x   along the tool's main moving axis: the direction the
         jaws travel on a gripper, the dispensing axis on a
         dispenser
     y   whatever completes the frame

   The world gets the same treatment: the WRF's z points up, out
   of the fixture (see 01_world_reference_frame).

   The payoff is that a tool pointing down at the work always
   reads the same way. Its z is the world's z turned upside down,
   so the first Euler angle is 180:

       MovePose(190, 0, 200, 180, 0, -90)
                             ---       ---
                  tool pointing down   which way the jaws face

   "180, 0, something" is the signature of a taught point. (The
   MecaPortal may read it back as -180. Same angle.) It is also
   what keeps relative moves readable: +z along the tool is
   always INTO the work, -z always away from it — see
   03_relative_moves.

   WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM

   Three sources, in increasing order of effort:
     - the tool's own drawing, if it was designed for this
     - measurement of the built assembly
     - a touch-off routine, jogging the tip to a known point in
       several orientations

   THE MEGP 25E, WORKED THROUGH

   On its standard adapter plate the MEGP 25E does not stick
   straight out of the flange. Its fingers point at right angles
   to it, along the FRF's x axis — which faces down when every
   joint is at zero. (That is why the zero position already looks
   ready to pick something up.) Its jaws travel along FRF y.

   So its TRF is moved AND turned:

       SetTrf(37, 0, 14, 0, 90, 90)

       37         along FRF x: 17 mm from the flange axis to the
                  finger mounting screw, plus 20 mm of finger
       14         along FRF z: flange face to that screw
       0, 90, 90  turns the frame so z runs out through the
                  fingertips and x along the jaws

   The 17 and the 14 are from the drawing in the MEGP 25* user
   manual. The 20 is a stand-in: Mecademic does not supply
   fingers — they are designed for the part — so measure yours
   and use it instead.

   This line is the house tool for every example from here on.

   WHY IT PAYS OFF LATER

   Change the gripper, change one line, and every pose in every
   program is still correct. Without a TRF, a new tool means
   reteaching every point. This is the argument that lands with
   anyone who maintains a cell rather than builds one.

   Like the WRF, the TRF resets to its default when the robot is
   activated or reactivated.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)
SetCartLinVel(60)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Before: no tool defined -----------------------------------
   TRF sits on the flange, so poses describe the flange: here,
   facing straight ahead, the way it does at zero. */
SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

MovePose(190, 0, 200, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

// Rotate 40 degrees about the TRF's z axis, then back. With no
// tool defined, that axis is the flange's own, pointing straight
// ahead — so the flange spins on the spot, and anything mounted
// on it sweeps through an arc. With an MEGP 25E fitted, watch
// the fingertips: 37 mm off that axis, they swing from side to
// side.
//
// MoveLin, not MovePose: a MoveLin keeps the TCP on a straight
// line — here, a line of zero length — so the pivot really does
// hold still for the whole move, not just at the two ends.
MoveLin(190, 0, 200, 0, 90, 40)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(190, 0, 200, 0, 90, -40)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(190, 0, 200, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)


/* --- After: the gripper defined --------------------------------
   The MEGP 25E's TRF, from the comments above. No physical tool
   is required to see the effect. */
SetTrf(37, 0, 14, 0, 90, 90)

// Move the FINGERTIPS to the same place the flange was before,
// pointing straight down. The arm visibly lifts and draws back
// to put them there, which on its own tells the story: the robot
// is now positioning the tool, not itself.
MovePose(190, 0, 200, 180, 0, -90)
Delay(1)

// The same 40 degrees about the TRF's z axis — which is now the
// tool's axis, pointing down through the fingertips. The
// fingertips stay put and the ARM swings around them. This is
// the moment the concept lands for most people; it is much more
// convincing seen than described.
//
// Because these fingers sit at right angles to the flange,
// turning them about their own axis is a big move for the arm:
// joint 1 and the wrist both swing. That is the cost of this
// mounting. (The MEGP 25E also comes with an optional 90 degree
// adapter plate — see its user manual.)
MoveLin(190, 0, 200, 180, 0, -50)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(190, 0, 200, 180, 0, -130)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(190, 0, 200, 180, 0, -90)


// --- Put the tool back ----------------------------------------
SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run the two halves back to back with no narration. "Watch
      what the tip does" is the only line you need.

   2. Change the 37 in SetTrf to 57 — fingers 20 mm longer — and
      run again. Longer tool, same code, still pivots about the
      tip. Then ask what changing a tool length costs them today.

   3. If a real gripper is mounted, measure its fingers, put the
      real length into SetTrf, and touch the tip to a fixed
      point. Rotate in place and show the tip staying on that
      point through the rotation. This is the single most
      persuasive thing you can do with a Meca500 on a table.

   NEXT   03_relative_moves.mxprog — moving along the tool axis,
          which is what the TRF really buys you.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
